      content:
          '- **Pain Duration**: 2 years bilateral heel pain. Right worse than left. Onset insidious — no specific injury.\n- **Pain Character**: Sharp, stabbing pain at the medial calcaneal tubercle bilaterally. Worst with first steps in the morning (9/10 right, 7/10 left). Improves to 5/10 with walking, then worsens to 8/10 after prolonged standing. Better with rest and non-weight-bearing.\n- **Aggravating Factors**: Prolonged standing (teaching), walking on hard surfaces, end of the day, barefoot walking.\n- **Relieving Factors**: Rest, ice, elevation, cushioned shoes.\n- **Failed Treatments**: (1) Over-the-counter gel insoles — minimal relief; (2) Stretching — \'tried it for a couple weeks, did not help\'; (3) Ibuprofen 600 mg TID x 2 weeks — mild improvement while taking, returned after stopping; (4) Corticosteroid injection bilateral heels 6 months ago — 3 weeks relief then pain returned.\n- **Functional Impact**: Limping by afternoon. Had to request a chair in her classroom (never sat before). Cannot walk her dog. Cannot exercise. Has gained 20 lbs since pain began (was BMI 34, now 38). Cannot keep up with students on field trips. Has missed 12 work days in the past year due to pain.\n- **PMH**: Obesity (BMI 38), type 2 diabetes (A1c 8.1%), hypertension, depression, bilateral knee osteoarthritis.\n- **Social**: Divorced. Two adult children. Lives alone. Elementary school teacher x 25 years. Enjoys (or enjoyed) walking, gardening, cooking.\n- **Weight History**: Struggled with weight since her 30s. Has tried multiple diets. Lost 40 lbs 5 years ago on a structured program but regained when she could no longer exercise due to foot pain. Feels defeated about weight management.\n- **Medications**: Lisinopril 20 mg daily, metformin 1000 mg BID, sertraline 100 mg daily, acetaminophen 1000 mg PRN.',
