          '**Vitals**: BP 138/86 mmHg, HR 78 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 98% on RA, Temp 36.7C, BMI 38, Weight 220 lbs.\n\n**General**: Overweight female who limps into the exam room favoring the right foot. Removes shoes slowly with discomfort. Pleasant and cooperative.\n\n**Feet**:\n- **Inspection**: Bilateral pes planus (flat feet). Mild hallux valgus bilaterally. Callousing of medial heel bilaterally. No erythema or swelling. Intact skin.\n- **Palpation**: Exquisite point tenderness at the medial calcaneal tubercle bilaterally (right > left). Pain reproduced with deep palpation. Tenderness extends along the medial band of the plantar fascia on the right. Windlass test positive bilaterally (dorsiflexion of great toe reproduces heel pain).\n- **ROM**: Ankle dorsiflexion with knee extended — 5 degrees bilaterally (normal >10 degrees — tight gastrocnemius-soleus complex). Dorsiflexion with knee flexed — 10 degrees (improved — confirms gastrocnemius tightness as primary contributor).\n- **Strength**: Ankle plantarflexion 5/5 bilaterally. Ankle dorsiflexion 5/5 bilaterally. Toe flexors 4+/5 bilaterally. Intrinsic foot muscles — mild weakness bilaterally.\n- **Arch**: Collapses with weight-bearing. Navicular drop test positive (>10 mm drop bilaterally).\n\n**Gait**: Antalgic gait favoring right. Reduced push-off bilaterally. Overpronation throughout stance phase. Short stride length.\n\n**Knees**: Bilateral crepitus with ROM. Mild medial joint line tenderness bilaterally. No effusion. ROM 0-130 degrees bilaterally.\n\n**Neurovascular**: Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses 2+ bilaterally. Sensation intact to monofilament (important — diabetic foot screening). No signs of peripheral neuropathy.\n\n**Imaging (brought by patient)**: Bilateral foot X-rays from 6 months ago — bilateral calcaneal spurs (incidental finding — not the cause of pain). No fractures. No evidence of stress fracture.',
