    Section(
      title: '+ Discuss the biomechanical factors contributing to her chronic plantar fasciitis, the differential diagnosis, and the stepwise treatment approach.',
      content:
          '**Biomechanical Contributors**:\n1. **Obesity (BMI 38)**: Strong association with plantar fasciitis. Increased mechanical load — the plantar fascia bears approximately 2-3x body weight during walking. At 220 lbs, her fascia experiences approximately 440-660 lbs of force with each step.\n2. **Pes planus (flat feet)**: Excessive pronation increases tensile strain on the plantar fascia along its medial band.\n3. **Gastrocnemius tightness**: Dorsiflexion limited to 5 degrees (need >10 degrees for normal gait). Tight calf muscles force compensatory pronation and increase plantar fascia strain. This is one of the **most important modifiable factors**.\n4. **Prolonged standing**: 6-8 hours/day on hard surfaces. Cumulative microtrauma.\n5. **Inadequate footwear**: Teacher — may be wearing non-supportive shoes.\n\n**Differential Diagnosis of Chronic Heel Pain**:\n1. **Plantar fasciitis** (most likely — classic presentation): First-step pain, medial calcaneal tubercle tenderness, positive Windlass test.\n2. **Calcaneal stress fracture**: Consider if pain is not strictly at the medial tubercle. Squeeze test (medial-lateral calcaneal compression) would be positive. MRI to evaluate.\n3. **Tarsal tunnel syndrome**: Tibial nerve entrapment behind medial malleolus. Burning, tingling in the sole. Tinel sign at tarsal tunnel. EMG/NCS for confirmation.\n4. **Fat pad atrophy**: Central heel pain (not medial). More common in elderly. Reduced subcutaneous tissue on palpation.\n5. **Baxter neuropathy (inferior calcaneal nerve entrapment)**: First branch of the lateral plantar nerve. Pain at medial heel, can mimic plantar fasciitis. Consider if typical treatment fails.\n6. **Calcaneal spur**: Present on X-ray but NOT the cause of pain — calcaneal spurs are present in 15-25% of the asymptomatic population. The spur is at the origin of the flexor digitorum brevis, not the plantar fascia.\n7. **Plantar fascia rupture**: Especially after steroid injection. Acute worsening of pain with palpable defect.\n8. **Seronegative spondyloarthropathy**: Bilateral heel pain in a woman — consider ankylosing spondylitis or reactive arthritis. Ask about back pain, morning stiffness. Check HLA-B27, ESR, CRP if clinical suspicion.\n\n**Evidence-Based Treatment Algorithm**:\n\n**Tier 1 — Correct the Biomechanics (she has not done this adequately)**:\n1. **Custom orthotic insoles**: NOT over-the-counter gel insoles. Custom semi-rigid orthotics with medial arch support and cushioned heel post. Address her pes planus and overpronation. This is the most important intervention she has not received.\n2. **Gastrocnemius stretching program** (the single most evidence-supported exercise): Wall stretch with knee extended, 30-second holds, 3 repetitions, 3 times daily. Compliance is key — she did it for 2 weeks. Need sustained 6-8 week commitment. Also: eccentric calf stretches on a step.\n3. **Plantar fascia-specific stretching**: Cross-leg stretch — cross affected foot over opposite knee, pull toes into dorsiflexion, stretching the fascia. Hold 10 seconds, 10 repetitions before first steps in the morning.\n4. **Footwear modification**: Supportive shoes with cushioned heel and arch support worn at ALL times — including in the house (no barefoot walking).\n5. **Night splint**: Dorsiflexion night splint (maintains ankle at 0-5 degrees dorsiflexion overnight, preventing fascial contracture). Compliance is challenging but evidence supports efficacy.\n\n**Tier 2 — Advanced Conservative**:\n6. **Extracorporeal shockwave therapy (ESWT)**: Strong evidence for chronic plantar fasciitis failing 6 months of conservative treatment. Radial or focused shockwave. Typically 3-5 sessions. NNT ~4 for significant improvement.\n7. **Physical therapy — targeted program**: Intrinsic foot strengthening (towel curls, marble pickups), progressive loading of the plantar fascia (heel raises progressing to single-leg), and manual therapy.\n8. **Corticosteroid injection**: She had one round — repeat injections increase risk of plantar fascia rupture and fat pad atrophy. Use sparingly.\n\n**Tier 3 — Advanced Interventional**:\n9. **Platelet-rich plasma (PRP) injection**: Emerging evidence — may be superior to corticosteroid for long-term outcomes.\n10. **Ultrasound-guided percutaneous tenotomy (Tenex)**: Minimally invasive debridement of degenerative fascia tissue.\n11. **Surgical plantar fasciotomy**: Last resort — partial plantar fascia release. Reserved for failure of all conservative measures after 12 months. Risk of arch collapse.',
