    Section(
      title: '+ Create an integrated management plan addressing foot pain, weight management, and the exercise-pain cycle.',
      content:
          '**Comprehensive Treatment Plan**:\n\n**1. Break the Pain-Inactivity-Weight Cycle**:\n- Her central problem: foot pain prevents exercise, lack of exercise causes weight gain, weight gain worsens foot pain. We must intervene at every point simultaneously.\n\n**2. Foot Pain Treatment (Aggressive Multi-Modal)**:\n- **Custom orthotics**: Prescription for custom semi-rigid foot orthotics from a certified orthotist. Medial arch support with deep heel cup and cushioned posting. Wear in all shoes.\n- **Gastrocnemius stretching**: Demonstrate in clinic. Written home program. 30-second holds, 3 reps, 3 times daily. Set phone reminders. This must be done for minimum 8 weeks consistently.\n- **Plantar fascia-specific stretch**: Before getting out of bed every morning — 10 repetitions of cross-leg toe dorsiflexion stretch.\n- **Night splint**: Prescribe dorsiflexion night splint. Explain that the first week is uncomfortable but most patients adapt. Use nightly for 3 months.\n- **ESWT referral**: Given 2 years of symptoms and failed conservative treatment, she qualifies for extracorporeal shockwave therapy. Refer to a center offering this.\n- **Footwear prescription**: Supportive athletic shoes (Brooks, ASICS, New Balance — brands with pronation control). Wear indoors and outdoors. No barefoot, no flip-flops, no flat shoes.\n- **Ice**: Frozen water bottle rolling under arch for 10-15 minutes after prolonged standing.\n\n**3. Non-Weight-Bearing Exercise Program (Start Immediately)**:\n- **Aquatic exercise**: Pool walking, water aerobics. Water eliminates ground reaction force — she can exercise without foot pain. 2-3 times weekly.\n- **Stationary cycling**: Minimal plantar fascia loading. Start 15 minutes at low resistance, progress.\n- **Seated upper body exercises**: Resistance bands, arm ergometer. Burns calories without foot loading.\n- **Chair yoga or seated fitness classes**: Maintain flexibility and core strength.\n- Goal: 150 minutes/week of moderate-intensity exercise through non-weight-bearing modalities while treating the foot pain.\n\n**4. Weight Management Referral**:\n- Refer to a multidisciplinary weight management program (dietitian, behavioral psychologist, exercise physiologist).\n- Discuss pharmacologic options: GLP-1 receptor agonists (semaglutide, tirzepatide) have dual benefit — weight loss AND improved diabetes control (her A1c is 8.1%). Coordinate with PCP.\n- Set realistic goals: 5-10% body weight loss (11-22 lbs) would significantly reduce plantar fascia loading and improve diabetes.\n\n**5. Workplace Modifications**:\n- **Anti-fatigue mat**: Standing mat at her teaching station.\n- **Seating**: Alternating sit-stand throughout the day (sit for 15 min every hour).\n- **Supportive shoes at work**: Must wear orthotics and supportive footwear during teaching.\n- **Brief stretch breaks**: 30-second calf stretch between classes.\n\n**6. Follow-Up Timeline**:\n- 4 weeks: Reassess pain, orthotic fit, stretching compliance, exercise tolerance.\n- 8 weeks: Evaluate response to conservative program. If insufficient, proceed with ESWT.\n- 3 months: Reassess comprehensively. If still failing, consider PRP injection or advanced interventions.\n- Ongoing: Weight management follow-up, diabetes monitoring.',
