      content:
          '**Systems-Level Approach**:\n\n**1. Structured Treatment Pathways**:\n- Implement evidence-based **clinical pathways** for common MSK conditions (plantar fasciitis, rotator cuff, low back pain) in primary care and orthopedic settings.\n- Pathway for plantar fasciitis should mandate: biomechanical assessment, appropriate orthotic prescription, stretching program education, and a minimum 6-8 week trial of comprehensive conservative treatment before injection.\n- This patient received an injection as a second visit intervention without ever receiving orthotics or a supervised stretching program — a common failure of fragmented care.\n\n**2. Physiatry as MSK Medical Home**:\n- PM&R physicians are ideally positioned to manage chronic MSK conditions because we address biomechanics, function, exercise, and pain holistically.\n- Referral to physiatry early in the course (not after 2 years of failed treatment) would have prevented chronicity.\n\n**3. Integrated Weight Management**:\n- Obesity is a modifiable risk factor for plantar fasciitis, knee OA, diabetes, and many other conditions. Healthcare systems should co-locate or integrate weight management services with MSK care.\n- The exercise-pain paradox (cannot exercise because of pain, cannot lose weight without exercise) should be recognized as a systems problem requiring multi-modal solutions.\n\n**4. Insurance Coverage Gaps**:\n- Custom orthotics: Often not covered or require excessive prior authorization. Advocate for coverage — orthotics are cost-effective compared to surgery.\n- ESWT: Variable coverage. May require appeal with documentation of failed conservative treatment.\n- Weight management programs: GLP-1 agonists are expensive. Ensure insurance covers evidence-based weight loss interventions as medical necessities.\n- Pool/aquatic therapy: May require specific prescription language to be covered.\n\n**5. Compliance Support**:\n- Stretching programs fail because patients are given a handout and sent home. Systems should include: video instruction, app-based exercise tracking, follow-up PT visits to verify technique, and accountability check-ins.\n- Night splint compliance is low — systems should include a follow-up call at 1 week to troubleshoot comfort issues.\n\n**6. Outcome Tracking**:\n- Track patient-reported outcomes (FAAM — Foot and Ankle Ability Measure) at each visit.\n- Monitor compliance with stretching, orthotic wear, and exercise program.\n- Use data to refine treatment pathways.',
